 the new images and all database schema upgrades and similar actions performed
 for you.
 
+Deployer checks
+---------------
+
+Each successful ``deploy``, ``deploy-containers``, ``reconfigure``,
+``upgrade`` and ``migrate-container-engine`` records what changed each host in
+``/etc/kolla/kolla-ansible-deployment.json`` on that host: the release, the
+Kolla Ansible version and git commit, the images' tag, the action and the
+time. The record is written whatever ``--tags`` were given.
+
+When Kolla Ansible runs from a containerised deployer image, which describes
+itself in ``/etc/kolla-ansible/deployer.json``, every action except
+``destroy`` first checks the deployer:
+
+* against the configuration: ``openstack_tag`` and ``openstack_release`` must
+  be the tag and release the deployer was built with;
+* against each target's record: a deployer for an older release, or for the
+  same release with an older Kolla Ansible version, is refused;
+* a deployer for a newer release may run only ``bootstrap-servers``, ``pull``,
+  ``prechecks``, ``gather-facts`` and ``upgrade`` until the upgrade is done;
+  and
+* ``upgrade`` may move to the next release, or from a ``.1`` release to the
+  next ``.1`` release (a SLURP upgrade). An upgrade to ``master`` is allowed
+  with a warning.
+
+``master`` counts as newer than every release, so a host last deployed from
+``master`` refuses a deployer for a stable release.
+
+Kolla Ansible installed with ``pip`` cannot tell which release it is for,
+because ``openstack_release`` is set in ``globals.yml``, so it writes the
+record but does not check it.
+
+``--override-deployer-checks`` turns each refusal into a warning, for example
+after an upgrade done by hand outside these rules.
+
 
 CLI Command Completion
 ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
